#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# break

#% text
# `break()` gives `failure(break)`, which ends the innermost iteration around it: a `while` or `for` loop (whose value is then `null`), or an iterating function such as `map`, `table`, `sum`, `select`, `fold` or `nest_while`, whose result then holds what was produced before it.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Like every failure, `failure(break)` ends the blocks and strict calls between it and the iteration.

#% item
# The iterating functions that read it: `map`, `map_indexed`, `map_thread`, `array` (of one dimension), `table`, `sum` and `product` with explicit ranges, `scan`, `select`, `select_first`, `all_true`, `any_true`, `none_true`, `fold`, `fold_list`, `nest`, `nest_list`, `nest_while`, `nest_while_list`, `fixed_point_list`, and `parallel_map` and `parallel_table` (with the serial result).

#% item
# Elements after the `break()` are not evaluated: their effects (`print`, `sow`) do not happen.

#% item
# Outside any iteration it stays a failure value.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
{ var i = 1; while true { if i > 10 then break(); i = i + 1 }; i }
#% output
#= 11

#% cell
{ var s = []; for k in range(10) { if k^2 > 20 then break(); s = append(s, k) }; s }
#% output
#= [1, 2, 3, 4]

#% cell
map(i => if i == 3 then break() else i, range(5))
#% output
#= [1, 2]

#% cell
table(if k^2 > 20 then break() else k, [k, 1, 10])
#% output
#= [1, 2, 3, 4]

#% cell
fold((a, b) => if a > 10 then break() else a + b, 0, range(10))
#% output
#= 15

#% cell
break()
#% output
#! failure(break)

